WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:03.800
Peter Parker continues to have bad luck with
his parents.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.660
While his real mom and dad are long gone,
Spider-Man's real-world guardians Disney and

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.740
Sony have been disputing over how best to
look after him.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.700
The web-flinging vigilante is a member of
the Marvel Cinematic Universe thanks to two

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.400
Tom Holland-starring films - 2017's Spider-Man:
Homecoming and 2019's Spider-Man: Far From

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.260
Home.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.500
He's also made a handful of appearances in
the MCU's ensemble movies - but he's always

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.340
remained the property of Sony Pictures.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.539
For months now, Disney, Marvel Studios, and
Sony have been in talks over how to split

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:34.699
financial stakes in the Spider-Man franchise
as they worked to update the shared custody

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.500
deal they struck in 2015.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.550
That landmark agreement allowed Marvel to
have access to the Spider-Man character while

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.020
Sony retained ownership.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:46.699
The stand-off reportedly ended on August 20,
when, according to Deadline, the two companies

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.140
failed to reach an agreement.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.580
The inability to strike new terms in a deal,
which would have seen Disney obtaining a co-financing

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:57.319
stake in the property, means that Marvel Studios
president Kevin Feige will no longer produce

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.380
Spider-Man films.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.680
Furthermore, Marvel will cease any involvement
in the Holland-starring Spidey universe moving

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.220
forward - meaning that Spider-Man is swinging
away from the MCU and crawling back into Sony's

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.000
web.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.640
Deadline's sources indicated that the talks
ended because of a disagreement over money.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.160
Disney is said to have requested a 50 percent
co-financing stake in future Spider-Man films

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:22.170
- of which there are reportedly two, anticipated
to feature both actor Tom Holland, and Jon

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.220
Watts returning in the director's chair.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.180
The talks also involved Disney potentially
extending that stake into other Spider-Man-adjacent

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.260
films, like the growing Venom franchise.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.270
A 50 percent stake is a hefty increase from
Marvel Studios' current share of the Spider-Man

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.450
movie series, which is "five percent of first
dollar gross" - or the money that a film makes

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.300
during its first day out in theaters.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.079
Sony wanted to keep that deal intact, Disney
didn't, and so Disney came forward with its

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.000
own proposition.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.619
As Deadline's Mike Fleming Jr. put it, Sony
executives, quote, "turned that offer down

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.080
flat" - apparently not even approaching Disney
with a counteroffer.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.299
According to the report, the studio had no
interest in splitting ownership over what

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.960
has become its most commercially successful
franchise.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.180
It's probably not a coincidence that just
a day before this news broke, Spider-Man:

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:12.090
Far From Home officially edged out Skyfall
to become Sony's highest-earning movie ever.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:16.610
Kevin Feige, a big fan of Spider-Man, reportedly
had every intention of continuing to produce

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.200
movies featuring the teen hero if Disney and
Sony were able to renegotiate terms.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.780
Sony is clearly confident that the franchise
has a bright future ahead without Feige or

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.860
Marvel Studios involved in producing it.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.520
In addition to the now Sony-controlled Spider-Man
sequels, the studio is confirmed to be working

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:37.430
on a sequel to Venom directed by Andy Serkis,
a Morbius: The Living Vampire movie starring

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:40.280
Jared Leto, and a film centered on Kraven
the Hunter.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.780
According to Deadline, they're also still
working on that Black Cat/Silver Sable thing

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.520
they've been trying to get made forever.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.580
The big question surrounding this news is
how it will affect the MCU, which has heavily

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.140
featured Spider-Man in its overarching narrative
up to this point.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.640
In just a handful of movies, Holland's Spider-Man
went from a spunky kid who played pinch hitter

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.320
during one tense airport battle to a universe-travelling
hero who would put his life on the line to

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:04.540
save other people.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:09.780
Peter Parker also developed a deeply moving
dynamic with Tony Stark, serving as his protégé-on-the-rise

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:13.280
and standing as the closest thing to a son
that Tony ever had.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.000
For Marvel fans, now it looks like they're
both gone.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.700
It seems like Spider-Man will never again
cross paths with other MCU heroes, and will

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.640
instead spend his next few movies cozying
up to characters in Sony's Spidey universe.

